Summary: Disable a global low-level hook that stops answering, so input keeps working
Category: input
Kind: fix
Origin: crossover (ported here)
  Version: 25.1.1
  URL: https://media.codeweavers.com/pub/crossover/source/crossover-sources-25.1.1.tar.gz
  Files: server/hook.c, server/queue.c, server/user.h
  Changes: removes the hung hook with remove_hook(), where CX only cleared
    its proc and left the hook counts counting it. The hung-hook hunks
    only; CX's esync/msync hunks of queue.c are left out
  Note: Sikarugir 10.0_8 ships it: wineserver: 'disabling hung hook: tid
    %04x, proc %#lx'
Origin: wine-mac-builds
  Version: 10.0
  Changes: disable_hung_hook() walks the chain with LIST_FOR_EACH_ENTRY,
    since 11.x's hook.c has no get_first_hook()/HOOK_ENTRY()
Upstream: no
Why: A global low-level keyboard or mouse hook is called by sending a
 message to the thread that set it, and input waits up to 2 s for the
 answer. When a hook's thread doesn't answer in time, the hook is now
 removed (remove_hook()), so one stuck hook can't delay every key press and
 mouse event on the desktop until Wine considers the thread hung (5 s
 without reading messages). Windows 7 and later do the same: a low-level
 hook that times out is removed without notice. WineHQ 11.22 still only
 times out (send_hook_ll_message).
Programs: none known; programs that set a low-level hook and then stop
 reading messages (launchers, overlays, hotkey tools)
Messages: "disabling hung hook: tid %04x, proc %#lx" (wineserver's stderr,
 only at wineserver debug level 2 or more, e.g. wineserver -d2)
Uncertain: CrossOver 26 no longer carries this; why isn't known.
References: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/winmsg/lowlevelkeyboardproc (Remarks)
Status: 11.21: 2026-11-26: remove_hook() instead of clearing proc; not yet
 in a build
